Ladybird now turns the glyphs of sideways-lr text a quarter turn
counterclockwise, from the bottom-left corner of their fragment. Run in
Chrome 158, Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5, all three draw sideways-lr text
reading bottom to top with the glyphs’ tops facing left, as Ladybird now
does, and WPT’s sideways-inline-001 and text-shadow-sideways-001 now
pass. Blink’s LineRelativeRect::ComputeRelativeToPhysicalTransform()
maps line-relative coordinates to physical ones with a counterclockwise
turn for kSidewaysLr, and a clockwise one for the other vertical modes,
for the text and its decorations alike; Gecko marks such a text run
IsSidewaysLeft(), and nsTextFrame passes sidewaysLeft to the painter,
which turns it the same way.
